HOW TO RUN THIS DISK====================This disk is NOT a boot disk, and os9exec does not boot anything.  There isno kernel here and no OS9Boot file.  os9exec is the kernel: you point it at adisk with OS9DISK and name a program to run, and that disk is what OS-9 thensees as /dd -- its root and its home.  There is no established name for thatrole, which is why people reach for "boot disk"; this disk cannot bootanything.Most of the programs in CMDS and CMDS/GAMES need nothing but this disk.------------------------------------------------------Trying things before you fetch cio------------------------------------------------------If you run OS-9 you have cio, csl and math -- but the SDK may be on a shelfrather than to hand.  This is what works in the meantime, so you can lookaround the disk straight away:    a shell        sh, or bash    list a dir     ls    read a file    cat, or less    copy a file    cat one > twocp, mv, rm, mkdir and dir are starred -- they are the GNU ones, and theywant cio like the other starred programs.  Under os9exec the emulator hasits own copy, move, rename and makdir, which need nothing loaded at all:    os9exec -r icopy   <from> <to>    os9exec -r imakdir <dir>    os9exec -r move    <from> <to>    os9exec -r rename  <old> <new>Those are commands of the EMULATOR, not programs on the disk, so they aretyped where you start os9exec rather than at a shell prompt inside it.On your own hardware the equivalents are the ones you already have: copy,makdir, del, list and dir.cio, csl and math now SHIP on this disk, with Microware's permission, sothe starred programs run too and nothing here is special.  DOC/README-CIOhas the detail.The starred ones in DOC/INDEX use Microware's cio (see DOC/README-CIO).They run: the module is here.MAKE YOUR TERMINAL 80 COLUMNS BY 24 LINES----------------------------------------Do this before running anything full-screen.  It is not a preference and itis not something the programs can work around.OS-9 has no way to ASK how big your terminal is.  A program learns its sizefrom the termcap entry for TERM, and the entry for vt100 says 24 lines of 80columns -- so that is what every full-screen program here believes, whateveryour window is actually set to.  os9exec cannot help: it implements no screensize call at all.If your window is taller, the programs draw into the top 24 lines and thebottom is dead space -- untidy but harmless.  If it is SHORTER or NARROWER,the screen scrolls where the program does not expect it to and the displayfalls apart.  `life' is the clearest example: correct at 80x24, a mess atanything else.MEASURED, 2026-08-27, so this is not a warning anybody guessed at.  `life' wasdriven on a pseudo-terminal sized 40 columns by 12 lines and captured(tools/playtest.py, `size' directive).  At 80x24 the bottom row reads    Generation: 12                Cycles every 8 generations.and at 40x12 that same status line is written straight THROUGH the board:    Gene@..@@@: 3The mechanism is not scrolling.  `life' addresses its status line to line 24absolutely; a terminal shorter than that clamps the address to its own lastline, which is somewhere in the middle of the picture.  Nothing is lost offthe bottom -- it is overwritten in place, which is worse, because the boardstill looks like a board.AND CHANGING TERM WILL NOT HELP.  /dd/SYS/termcap holds exactly ONE entry:    xterm-256color|xterm|vt100|xterm with 256 colors:            :co#80:it#8:li#24:All three names are aliases for that one description, and it says 80 columnsby 24 lines outright.  So `TERM=vt100', `TERM=xterm' and `TERM=xterm-256color'all tell a program the same thing, whatever your window is really set to.  Terminal.app, iTerm, xterm:  SIZE THE WINDOW TO 80x24 before you start.  Or, if you would rather keep your window as it is, edit the `co#' and `li#'  numbers in /dd/SYS/termcap to match it.  That is the only thing that  changes what the programs believe.SOME PROGRAMS WANT TERMCAP TO BE THE ENTRY ITSELF-------------------------------------------------SYS/login sets TERMCAP to /dd/SYS/termcap -- the NAME OF THE FILE --and that is what about seventy-five programs here expect.  A handfulwere built against a different termcap library, one that reads TERMCAPas the CAPABILITY STRING itself.  Those get the pathname where theyexpected a terminal description, and stop:    'vt100': Unknown terminal typeIt reads like a broken program and it is not.  SYS/termcap.entryexists for exactly this -- source it, and they work:    . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry    gnuchessMEASURED 2026-08-29: gnuchess, gnuchessn and hexedit.  GNU Chess drawsits board, keeps both clocks and plays; hexedit opens its viewer.All three were recorded as broken before that, on a wrong reading ofthe message above as wanting a terminfo database.MEASURED AND DO NOT NEED IT, whatever tools/howto.psv says: `sc'opens its spreadsheet and `umacs' opens its buffer with TERMCAP leftas SYS/login sets it.  Still reported to want it and not re-measured:jargon in its interactive -m mode, and vi_cio.If a full-screen program here stops with `Unknown terminal type',this is the first thing to try.DO NOT set it permanently.  TERMCAP as a PATHNAME is what the otherseventy-odd termcap programs on this disk need, and setting it to theentry breaks them instead.  Source it in the shell where you want oneof the seven, and the rest of your session is unaffected.A FEW PROGRAMS NEED A MODULE LOADED FIRST----------------------------------------Most of this disk needs nothing but this disk.  A handful link a libraryMODULE rather than a file, and a module has to be in the module directorybefore it can be linked.  That is what `load' is for, and CMDS/load is onthis disk:    load /dd/CMDS/os9libIt is a clean-room reimplementation of Microware's utility, written fromthe published manuals and contributed by the os9exec project, with itssource in SRC/load.  It takes the five options that manual page documents-- -d, -l, -z, -z=<file> and -? -- and searches PATH the way the manualsays load does.  Built trap-free, so it needs no cio itself.What needs it: the RTF Fortran-77 system -- rtf, for, lnk, biory, creadoc --which links `os9lib'.  Without it they print NOTHING and exit, which readslike five broken programs and is not.  DOC/README-FORTRAN has the detail.Verified: with os9lib loaded, `for', `lnk' and `lnk.org' print their commandlines, `rtf' prints "command must be: RTF pathname [options..]", and `biory'prompts for a name and a birth date in German.  With nothing loaded all fiveare silent.  That silence is the whole symptom.The Atari `Graph' library and `VMod_trap' are a different matter and noamount of loading fixes them: both run in supervisor state.  Loaded, theyare found and entered, and the fault moves INTO the library -- a privilegeviolation on its own `RTE', which is a supervisor-only instruction.  Six ofthe seven Graph programs stop at the same address inside it.  DOC/STATUS sayswhich programs those are.SEVENTEEN PROGRAMS WANT A RAM DISK AT /r0-----------------------------------------OS-9's C library makes a temporary file at /r0 -- the RAM disk a real systemhas -- and twenty programs here carry that path inside them:  vi  me  ed  hexed  bash  hist  roff  pri  preset  rndir  expreserve  UUCP/mail  UUCP/expire  UUCP/rnews  UUCP/uucico  ELM/filter  GAMES/apfel  GAMES/graphsave  NETPBM/picttoppm  NETPBM/pnmsmoothOn your own OS-9 system /r0 is there and they work.  Under os9exec there isno way to make one: its `mount' creates devices named h0 through hz andnothing else, so a program that reaches for /r0 gets    /r0/ed.000003: 000:221 module not foundand stops.  Measured 2026-08-27 -- `ed' and `hexed' fail outright, and theothers fail only at the moment they need the temporary file, which for `vi'and `bash' is rarely and for `ed' is at once.Nothing on this disk can fix that; it is the shape of the emulator.  It islisted here so the message is recognisable when you meet it.WHERE THE PROGRAMS THINK THEY LIVE----------------------------------Some carry a hardcoded /h0 path.  That is not damage: they were built bypeople whose freeware lived on /h0.Most of those want just one file, /h0/sys/termcap -- and every one of themreads the TERMCAP environment variable first.  SYS/login sets it to thisdisk's own copy, so they work wherever the disk is mounted, with no /h0in sight.  That was measured, program by program, with no exceptions.What is left is a handful wanting data of their own under /h0, like/h0/games/hack/playground; mostly the gcc passes and the linker.  Goingthe other way, FAR MORE programs read their data from /dd --advent wants /dd/GAMES/adv/glorkz, fortune /dd/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes.dat,nroff /dd/LIB/tmac.*.No single arrangement satisfies both sets, which is what the three below arereally about.  DOC/DEPENDS lists every path each program opens.IF YOU WANT THE SHORT ANSWER: mount this collection as /dd, and hard-linkit as /h0 as well.  Far more programs want their data at /dd than at /h0 --the ratio is about five to one -- so /dd is where the collection belongs,and the extra link costs one inode and collects the rest.  That isarrangement 2.  For the figures as they stand today, runtools/measure_layout.py in the source repository.------------------------------------------------------------------1. YOU ALREADY RUN OS-9, AND WANT TO KEEP YOUR OWN /dd------------------------------------------------------------------Keep your own system as /dd and mount this collection as /h0:    OS9DISK=<your own disk>  OS9H0=<this image, named h0>  os9exec shellThen add this disk's commands to your path, in your own startup:    setenv PATH /dd/CMDS:/h0/CMDS:/h0/CMDS/GAMESPATH works in the OS-9 shell -- a bare command name is found along it, notonly in the execution directory.  (Verified: with /h0/CMDS on PATH a bare`rndname' runs; with it removed the same command gives E_PNNF.)What it buys you:  - /dd stays yours -- your CMDS, your LIB, your startup, your cio.  - Your own cio is used rather than the copy here, if you prefer it.  - Every hardcoded /h0 path resolves against this disk, where its data is.What it costs, and it is NOT a small cost: a large share of the programs readdata from /dd -- advent its cave, fortune its database, nroff its macrofiles -- and under this arrangement /dd is YOURS, so they will look onyour disk and not find them.  This is the single biggest reason toprefer arrangement 2.  If you want a few of them here anyway, copy whatDOC/DEPENDS lists for each onto your own /dd, keeping the same/dd-relative places.For a shell with this disk's commands, TERMCAP and a working cd already setup, hand bash the login script -- it works out where the disk is mountedfrom the path you give it:    os9exec -r bash /h0/SYS/loginThe image must be a file NAMED h0: os9exec resolves an image by filename, soOS9H0 pointing at a file called anything else fails with error 214.Two things to know:  - If /h0 is already YOUR disk, keep it that way and see arrangement 3.  - The image must stay writable.  hack, larn and cribbage write into    /h0/games/*/playground and criblog.------------------------------------------------------------------2. THIS DISK AS /dd  --  recommended------------------------------------------------------------------Run this disk by itself.  It carries public-domain shells, so no Microwaremodule is needed anywhere:    OS9DISK=<this image>  os9exec -r bash /dd/SYS/loginThat trailing /dd/SYS/login matters.  Started bare, bash has no PATH, noHOME and no TERM, so it finds no command; SYS/login exports those and handsover to an interactive shell.  Setting HOME is also what makes bash read/dd/.bashrc, where cd, pwd and the aliases are defined.SEVEN NAMES BASH KEEPS FOR ITSELF.  break, enable, fc, for, help, if andtrap are all programs in CMDS, and all seven are also bash builtins orkeywords.  The shell answers first and never says it did: `help' gives youbash's builtin list, not the .hlp system, and `for' starts a loop ratherthan the FORTRAN driver.  Give the path -- /dd/CMDS/help -- and they run.DOC/STATUS lists them with what each one really is.This gets you very nearly everything: every program in CMDS andCMDS/GAMES, starred or not, because the five Microware modules ship here;those that read their data from /dd, because /dd is this disk; and --because SYS/login sets TERMCAP -- the ones that would otherwise want/h0/sys/termcap.  Only those with other /h0 data are out of reach, andthey are mostly compiler passes.If you want those as well, give os9exec the image under a second name:    ln -s osk-freeware.dd h0    OS9DISK=<...>/osk-freeware.dd  OS9H0=<...>/h0  os9exec -r bash /dd/SYS/loginos9exec will not mount one path as two devices, so it needs two names; asymlink is enough and costs nothing -- verified 2026-08-29, `ls /h0'through a symlink to the image lists the disk.  Understand what it is, though: ONEimage behind two device names, each with its own sector cache.  Reading isfine.  Writing through both at once is not, so do not count on a scorewritten via /h0 being visible via /dd in the same session.Caveats when running the disk alone:  - PATH search works from the IMAGE and not from a host directory.  Run    the .dd and a bare `cookie' is found; point OS9DISK at an unpacked    directory instead and the same command says "command not found" with    PATH set and the program sitting there.  Reading .bashrc fails the same    way, with E$Unit.  Full paths always work.  - `sh', which the startup script names as its shell, starts but cannot    fork ANY external command.  It sees the file, agrees it is executable,    and the fork still fails.  Use bash.  - `ksh' CAN be the first shell, re-measured 2026-08-29.  The note here    said it could not -- that it wanted cio loaded and so needed a shell    first -- and that was true before the five Microware modules shipped    on this disk.  `os9exec -r /dd/CMDS/ksh' starts it, and it forks    external commands normally.  It reads no login script of its own,    so set PATH yourself or start it from bash.------------------------------------------------------------------3. TAKE ONLY WHAT YOU WANT------------------------------------------------------------------Nobody needs SNOBOL, FORTRAN and XLISP at once.  To move a few programs ontoyour own disk and keep your own /h0:  - Copy this disk's GAMES/ to your own disk, so /h0/games/... resolves    against yours.  That is what the game data paths expect.  - Read DOC/DEPENDS before copying any single program.  It lists, per    program, every absolute path that program carries -- generated by    scanning the binaries.  Many need something besides their own    binary: a dictionary, a skeleton file, a scratch directory.  Copying    just the command often is not enough.  - Your own /h0/sys/termcap is then the one that gets used, which is    probably better tuned for your terminal than ours.  Or set TERMCAP and    the question does not arise.------------------------------------------------------------------THE SHELL YOU GET------------------------------------------------------------------bash 1.12 with /dd/.bashrc read, which matters more than it sounds.ALWAYS HAND BASH THE LOGIN SCRIPT.  Started bare --`os9exec /h0/CMDS/bash' -- you get a shell with no PATH, so `ls' is notfound; no HOME, so .bashrc is never read and bash complains about/.bashrc; and a `cd' that ABORTS THE SHELL on first use.  Everythingbelow depends on SYS/login having run.With no OS9DISK set, /dd is a host directory rather than an RBF disk, andbash prints `shell-init: getwd: cannot access parent directories' once atstartup.  It is harmless and there is nothing to fix: SYS/login spends thesame failure deliberately, in a script where it cannot be fatal, which isexactly what leaves cd working afterwards.What you get:  - cd and pwd WORK, but only because .bashrc replaces them.  bash's own    getwd() walks `..' upward looking for a directory that is its own    parent.  OS-9 has one root per DEVICE and nothing above them, so that    walk never ends: plain `pwd' HANGS THE SHELL, and where /dd is not an    RBF device plain `cd' aborts it with a bus error.  If a terminal here    has ever had to be closed rather than exited, this is the likeliest    reason.  The replacements track the path by string instead.  - ls, cat, less, wc, basename and dirname all run with no cio.  dir,    edir and l are the Microware-style listers and are starred; ls is the    one to use.  - vi_nocio is the editor that needs nothing -- PVic, cio-free, and it    drives a vt100 without any termcap file at all.  USE IT, or `elvis'.    Plain `vi' is the EFFO build -- the genuine Berkeley ex/vi source --    and it PAINTS ONLY TWO LINES of a file here, the first line and a    half-drawn rule, whatever you do; vi_cio is starred.  Measured    2026-08-29.  - `exit' leaves.  So does `quit' in most of the games; read DOC/INDEX    before starting one you cannot get out of.DOC/INDEX says what each program is; DOC/ORIGINS says where it came from;SOURCES.txt records the terms it arrived under.